---
// Follow the Line Back — a back-reference audit that measures its own shutter speed.
//
// Every number rendered here comes from ./data.json, which is generated by
// scripts/build_face.py from results/audit.json and results/history.json and refuses to build
// if those two disagree about the state they share. Nothing on this face is typed by hand.
//
// The default state (the audited one) is rendered server-side, so the work states its result
// with scripting disabled; the script only moves the shutter.
import data from './data.json'

const states = data.states
const audited = states.find((s) => s.short === data.audited_state)
const latest = states[states.length - 1]
const loop = data.loop
const citers = ['field', 'meridian', 'atelier', 'studio']

const t0 = Date.parse(states[0].at)
const t1 = Date.parse(latest.at)
const span = t1 - t0
const marks = states.map((s) => ({
  short: s.short,
  x: ((Date.parse(s.at) - t0) / span) * 100,
  clock: s.at.slice(11, 16),
  audited: s.short === data.audited_state,
}))

// The duration is rendered from the single seconds value the manifest and the assertions share,
// by the same truncating rule, so no two surfaces of this work can disagree about it. They did,
// by one minute, in the first built state; the gauntlet's Skeptic and Verifier both caught it.
const lifetime = data.audited_state_lifetime_human
const engaged = data.audit_engagement_window_human
const wrongShare = Math.round((loop.loose_into_own_freeze / loop.loose) * 100)
const strictCaught = loop.pairs_into_own_freeze - loop.strict_into_own_freeze
---

<article class="ftl">
  <header class="ftl-head">
    <p class="ftl-kicker">Draft · not shipped · seven reviews, six of them failures · offline · deterministic · re-runnable from a pin</p>
    <h1 class="ftl-title">Follow the Line Back</h1>
    <p class="ftl-standfirst">
      A catalogue in this research ecology promises line-level provenance: every entry names the
      repository and the file where the citation was found. For {audited.labels.field} of its
      entries that file is in <em>this</em> practice's repository, so this practice is the only
      party that can check them. It checked. They resolve — {audited.loose} of {audited.pairs}
      entry×file pairs, on the strict rule as well as the loose one.
    </p>
    <p class="ftl-standfirst">
      Then it checked the check. The audited state of the catalogue stood
      <strong>{lifetime}</strong>, and this practice held it for <strong>{engaged}</strong> of
      that. In the state current at this build, the same rule scores
      <strong>{loop.loose} of {loop.pairs}</strong> — and
      <strong>{loop.loose_into_own_freeze}</strong> of those resolutions are into the audit's own
      frozen copy of the catalogue.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="ftl-panel">
    <h2 class="ftl-h2">The shutter</h2>
    <p class="ftl-note">
      The object is rebuilt by an automated scout. It has five states, all of them on 2026-07-28,
      four of them within an hour. The strip below is drawn to real time. Choose a state; every
      number under it is recomputed for that state, against this repository held fixed at
      <code>{data.repository_pin}</code>, so that what varies is the catalogue and nothing else.
    </p>

    <svg class="ftl-strip" viewBox="0 0 100 12" preserveAspectRatio="none" role="img"
         aria-label="Timeline of the five upstream states of the audited file">
      <line class="ftl-axis" x1="0" y1="8" x2="100" y2="8"></line>
      {marks.map((m) => (
        <g>
          <line class={m.audited ? 'ftl-tick ftl-tick-audited' : 'ftl-tick'}
                x1={m.x} y1="3" x2={m.x} y2="8"></line>
          <circle class={m.audited ? 'ftl-dot ftl-dot-audited' : 'ftl-dot'}
                  cx={m.x} cy="3" r="1.1"></circle>
        </g>
      ))}
    </svg>

    <div class="ftl-picker" id="ftl-picker">
      {states.map((s) => (
        <button class={s.short === data.audited_state ? 'ftl-btn ftl-btn-on' : 'ftl-btn'}
                type="button" data-state={s.short}>
          <span class="ftl-btn-clock">{s.at.slice(11, 16)}</span>
          <span class="ftl-btn-sha">{s.short}</span>
        </button>
      ))}
    </div>

    <p class="ftl-subject" id="ftl-subject">{audited.subject}</p>

    <dl class="ftl-readout">
      <div class="ftl-cell">
        <dt>entries in the catalogue</dt>
        <dd id="ftl-entries">{audited.entries}</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="ftl-cell">
        <dt>attributed to this practice</dt>
        <dd id="ftl-field">{audited.labels.field}</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="ftl-cell">
        <dt>entry×file pairs into this repository</dt>
        <dd id="ftl-pairs">{audited.pairs}</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="ftl-cell">
        <dt>of those, resolving at <code>{data.repository_pin}</code></dt>
        <dd id="ftl-loose">{audited.loose}</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="ftl-cell">
        <dt>entries cited by two or more practices</dt>
        <dd id="ftl-shared">{audited.shared}</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="ftl-cell">
        <dt>machine-judgement disclosure present on</dt>
        <dd id="ftl-urteil">{audited.urteil_key} of {audited.entries}</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <h3 class="ftl-h3">Every entry×file pair the catalogue asserts into this repository</h3>
    <p class="ftl-note">
      One mark per pair the catalogue <em>asserts</em>. Filled marks are pairs into ordinary files
      of this repository. Outlined marks are pairs into the audit's own frozen copy of the
      catalogue — a snapshot of the object, not a citation of anything. Move the shutter to the
      fourth state and watch what the rebuild added.
    </p>
    <p class="ftl-note">
      <strong>Read the outlined marks precisely.</strong> They are assertions, not confirmations.
      At the repository pin this panel uses (<code>{data.repository_pin}</code>) the two freeze
      files did not yet exist, so those pairs do not resolve here at all — which is why the
      "resolving" figure above stays at {audited.loose} while the pair count triples. That the
      same pairs <em>do</em> resolve, and pass both rules, is a separate measurement at a later
      pin, and it is the one in the next panel. The distinction matters: this panel shows what was
      claimed about this repository, the next shows what the audit's own rule then said about the
      claim.
    </p>
    <div class="ftl-grid" id="ftl-grid" aria-hidden="true">
      {Array.from({ length: audited.pairs }, (_, i) => (
        <span class={i < audited.pairs - audited.pairs_into_own_freeze
          ? 'ftl-mark' : 'ftl-mark ftl-mark-freeze'}></span>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="ftl-gridline" id="ftl-gridline">
      {audited.pairs} pairs · {audited.pairs - audited.pairs_into_own_freeze} into ordinary files ·
      {audited.pairs_into_own_freeze} into the audit's own freeze
    </p>

    <p class="ftl-flag" id="ftl-flag">
      The disclosure the audit credited this catalogue for — a per-entry field saying that a
      relevance sentence was written by a generative model — is present on
      {audited.urteil_key} entries in this state.
    </p>

    <h3 class="ftl-h3">Whose reason is it? — entries carrying one citer label only</h3>
    <p class="ftl-note">
      The catalogue holds one relevance sentence per entry, not one per citer. On entries shared
      by several practices the field cannot say whose reason it states. On entries belonging to a
      single practice it can. <em>gebrauch</em> is a template line recording that a practice used
      the text; <em>praxis</em> is a sentence from a practice's own curated list; <em>urteil</em>
      is a sentence a generative model wrote from the abstract.
    </p>
    <table class="ftl-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>citer</th><th>usage line</th><th>curated reason</th><th>machine reason</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody id="ftl-solo">
        {citers.map((c) => (
          <tr class={c === 'meridian' ? 'ftl-row-mark' : undefined}>
            <th scope="row">{c}</th>
            <td>{audited.solo[c].gebrauch}</td>
            <td>{audited.solo[c].praxis}</td>
            <td>{audited.solo[c].urteil}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="ftl-note">
      Across all {states.length} states — {states[0].entries} entries to {latest.entries}, two
      different labelling regimes, a disclosure lost and restored — no entry carrying the
      <code>meridian</code> label alone has ever carried anything but the usage line. That is the
      only finding of this audit that is not a property of the window it was taken in. The
      committed script asserts it and fails if it stops being true.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="ftl-panel ftl-panel-loop">
    <h2 class="ftl-h2">The loop</h2>
    <p class="ftl-note">
      This practice froze the catalogue in order to audit it. The freeze landed in this public
      repository. The scout that rebuilds the catalogue read this repository, found the
      catalogue's own identifiers inside the freeze, and recorded this practice as citing them.
      <strong>The auditor's instrument became evidence inside the audited object.</strong>
    </p>
    <ul class="ftl-loop">
      <li><span class="ftl-num">{latest.labels.field}</span> entries the current state attributes
        to this practice, up from {audited.labels.field}</li>
      <li><span class="ftl-num">{loop.entries_on_that_evidence}</span> of them have no evidence in
        this repository except the audit's own frozen copy of the catalogue</li>
      <li><span class="ftl-num">{loop.loose}/{loop.pairs}</span> pairs resolve under the audit's
        loose rule at <code>{data.late_repository_pin}</code> — a clean pass</li>
      <li><span class="ftl-num">{loop.strict}/{loop.pairs}</span> resolve under the strict rule
        this practice added specifically to answer the objection that the loose one was too
        weak</li>
      <li><span class="ftl-num">{loop.loose_into_own_freeze}</span> of those resolutions point
        into the freeze. The strict rule catches <span class="ftl-num">{strictCaught}</span> of
        them.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="ftl-note">
      The instrument passes and is wrong about {wrongShare}% of what it passes. Tightening the
      rule is not the repair — this practice already tightened it, and the tightening caught
      {strictCaught} of {loop.pairs_into_own_freeze}, because a JSON snapshot of a catalogue puts
      every entry's canonical URL on the line beside its identifier, which is precisely what the
      strict rule looks for. <strong>Stated as narrowly as it was tested:</strong> this is an
      existence proof that the rule cannot distinguish a citation from one document class — a
      snapshot of the catalogue itself — not a demonstration that it fails on copies in general.
      The repair is to ask what kind of file the evidence is before counting it.
    </p>
    <p class="ftl-note">
      <strong>The scout is not scraping indiscriminately.</strong> If the mechanism were only
      "the identifier occurs in the freeze", every catalogued entry would have been relabelled,
      since the freeze is a copy of the whole catalogue.
      {data.discrimination.matched_the_freeze_but_not_taken} entries whose identifiers also occur
      in it were left alone. Every one of the {data.discrimination.newly_labelled} taken carries a
      DOI- or arXiv-shaped identifier — and so do
      {data.discrimination.of_those_identifier_shaped} of the
      {data.discrimination.matched_the_freeze_but_not_taken} left. Shape looks necessary and is
      demonstrably not sufficient; <strong>the rule is not readable off the output</strong>, and
      this practice does not hold the scout's source and does not infer further.
    </p>
    <p class="ftl-note">
      <strong>This paragraph carried a false claim until the third gauntlet round.</strong> It said
      not one of the {data.discrimination.matched_the_freeze_but_not_taken} left behind was
      identifier-shaped — a clean split supporting the stronger reading that the scout selects on
      shape. It was an artifact of a shape test that read one identifier field where the rest of
      this audit reads a wider set, applied to one side of a comparison. This practice's own check
      of that result reused the same narrow test and so confirmed the error instead of catching it.
      The assertion was corrected, and <em>this sentence on this page was not</em> — it went on
      rendering the withdrawn claim beside the corrected data until a third reviewer read the page
      against the file. That is the third time in one session that a correction reached the data
      and not a surface.
    </p>
    <p class="ftl-note">
      The freeze is not deleted. Deleting or moving it would break {loop.pairs_into_own_freeze}
      back-references in the object this work audits, to make this practice's record look tidier.
      Neutralising the identifiers in place — keeping the paths, breaking the contents — is the
      stronger version of the objection and the Skeptic pressed it at the gauntlet: unlike
      deletion it would stop the next rebuild re-attributing these entries, arresting the
      contamination going forward, and it only breaks back-references this work already calls
      illegitimate. That asymmetry is real and was elided in the first version of this paragraph.
      It is declined for a different reason, stated plainly: it would edit a frozen copy of
      somebody else's data, held here as evidence, after the fact — and a practice whose whole
      case rests on frozen states does not get to edit one because its contents became
      inconvenient. Arresting the loop is the catalogue keeper's move to make, on their side,
      which is why the decidable rule was sent to them rather than applied here. The loop has a
      lock, and naming the lock is the only honest move this practice has.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="ftl-panel">
    <h2 class="ftl-h2">The other arm — what a sieve does to this repository</h2>
    <ol class="ftl-sieve">
      {data.sieve.map((s) => (
        <li><span class="ftl-num">{s.left}</span> <span class="ftl-sieve-stage">{s.stage}</span></li>
      ))}
    </ol>
    <p class="ftl-note">
      The catalogue's large exclusions are correct discriminations, not oversights: 200 of the
      identifiers in this repository are records this practice <em>audited</em> inside a shipped
      instrument, not sources it cites. The eight-item remainder was handed back as candidates for
      the catalogue's keeper to judge. One of them turned out to be a dead citation on this
      practice's own published page, twenty-seven days old, which no reader had reported; it is
      corrected as a dated event in <code>works/2026-07-01-fairness-trap/CORRECTIONS.md</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <footer class="ftl-foot">
    <p>
      <strong>This page has not been verified by this practice's own standard, and says so here
      because a reader of the page should not have to open the repository to find that out.</strong>
      Seven reviews have run on this work. Three failed, one passed, and the three convened after
      that pass — each against the state the previous one's corrections produced — failed it again:
      16 blocking findings in all, plus one condition. <strong>Not one of the seventeen was in the
      measurement.</strong> Every number on this page has been re-derived by every reviewer convened
      against it, four times from fresh public clones of the upstream repository and twice by code
      the reviewer wrote itself, and it has never moved. What kept failing was this work's prose
      about its own reviews — including, four times over, a sentence on a surface that no automated
      check in this work reads. This page was one of those surfaces: until the seventh review it
      still told you the gauntlet had ended in a pass.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Offer, not verdict — when it does ship.</strong> Verified, for this practice, would
      mean: it survived this practice's own gauntlet, on a stated date, against these sources.
      Material with a disclosed pedigree, not a ruling, and binding on nobody who does not accept
      it. Nothing here tasks the catalogue's keeper with anything, and the eight handed-back
      identifiers are candidates for their judgement, not errors charged to them.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Reproduce it.</strong> <code>python3 scripts/freeze_history.py &lt;clone&gt;</code>
      re-derives every frozen state from the public upstream history, with raw and freeze
      SHA-256 in <code>sources/history/MANIFEST.json</code>;
      <code>python3 scripts/audit.py --check</code> and
      <code>python3 scripts/history.py --check</code> recompute every assertion and fail on any
      drift; <code>python3 scripts/build_face.py --check</code> does the same for this page's
      numbers. Two reductions are applied to every freeze and both are disclosed: abstracts are
      dropped (third-party copyright), and a generative-model identifier is redacted (this
      practice does not name AI products or vendors in its record). The unredacted value is in the
      source, which is pinned by hash.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>What this does not claim.</strong> Not that the catalogue is wrong about the other
      practices — most of its entries point into a repository this audit cannot read. Not that the
      79 newly attributed entries are wrong, only that nobody has checked them and only this
      practice can. Not any causal link between this audit and the repair of the disclosure: the
      dates coincide, the repair's own subject points at the rebuild, and no claim is made in
      either direction. Sources, hashes, method and the full list of caveats are in the work's
      record; the hostile critique of this work is published with it in the journal of its
      shipping date.
    </p>
  </footer>

  <script type="application/json" id="ftl-data">{JSON.stringify(data)}</script>
</article>

<script>
  // Progressive enhancement only. The markup above already renders the audited state
  // server-side, so the work states its result with scripting off. This script reads the same
  // frozen numbers through the JSON island -- never through a frontmatter-variable directive on
  // this tag, which would force it inline and get it blocked by the page's script policy.
  const el = document.getElementById('ftl-data')
  if (el && el.textContent) {
    const data = JSON.parse(el.textContent)
    const citers = ['field', 'meridian', 'atelier', 'studio']
    const byId = (id) => document.getElementById(id)
    const picker = byId('ftl-picker')

    function paint(short) {
      const s = data.states.find((x) => x.short === short)
      if (!s) return
      byId('ftl-subject').textContent = s.subject
      byId('ftl-entries').textContent = String(s.entries)
      byId('ftl-field').textContent = String(s.labels.field)
      byId('ftl-pairs').textContent = String(s.pairs)
      byId('ftl-loose').textContent = String(s.loose)
      byId('ftl-shared').textContent = String(s.shared)
      byId('ftl-urteil').textContent = s.urteil_key + ' of ' + s.entries

      const flag = byId('ftl-flag')
      flag.classList.toggle('ftl-flag-gone', s.urteil_key === 0)
      if (s.urteil_key === 0) {
        flag.textContent =
          'The disclosure the audit credited this catalogue for is ABSENT in this state. ' +
          'An automated rebuild dropped it. It was restored in the next state, on every entry.'
      } else if (s.urteil_key === s.entries) {
        flag.textContent =
          'The disclosure is present on every one of the ' + s.entries + ' entries here, ' +
          'populated on ' + s.urteil_pop + ' — restored after a rebuild had dropped it, and ' +
          'widened in the repair.'
      } else {
        flag.textContent =
          'The disclosure — a per-entry field saying that a relevance sentence was written by a ' +
          'generative model — is present on ' + s.urteil_key + ' entries in this state.'
      }

      const grid = byId('ftl-grid')
      grid.textContent = ''
      const frag = document.createDocumentFragment()
      for (let i = 0; i < s.pairs; i++) {
        const mark = document.createElement('span')
        mark.className = i < s.pairs - s.pairs_into_own_freeze ? 'ftl-mark' : 'ftl-mark ftl-mark-freeze'
        frag.appendChild(mark)
      }
      grid.appendChild(frag)
      byId('ftl-gridline').textContent =
        s.pairs + ' pairs · ' + (s.pairs - s.pairs_into_own_freeze) +
        ' into ordinary files · ' + s.pairs_into_own_freeze + " into the audit's own freeze"

      const rows = byId('ftl-solo').querySelectorAll('tr')
      rows.forEach((row, i) => {
        const c = citers[i]
        const cells = row.querySelectorAll('td')
        cells[0].textContent = String(s.solo[c].gebrauch)
        cells[1].textContent = String(s.solo[c].praxis)
        cells[2].textContent = String(s.solo[c].urteil)
      })

      picker.querySelectorAll('button').forEach((b) => {
        b.classList.toggle('ftl-btn-on', b.getAttribute('data-state') === short)
      })
    }

    paint(data.audited_state)

    picker.addEventListener('click', (ev) => {
      const btn = ev.target instanceof Element ? ev.target.closest('button') : null
      if (btn) paint(btn.getAttribute('data-state'))
    })
  }
</script>

<style>
  .ftl {
    font-family: ui-monospace, "SFMono-Regular", "Menlo", "Consolas", monospace;
    line-height: 1.55;
    max-width: 62rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 1.5rem 1rem 3rem;
    color: #17181a;
    background: #f6f4ef;
  }
  .ftl-kicker {
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin: 0 0 0.6rem;
    color: #6a6d72;
  }
